  "final_text": "**No, not on these numbers. Rework CRAFT first.** The VP's case rests on three claims. When I recompute each one from the CSVs on a like-for-like basis, it either goes away or reverses.\n\n## The VP's three claims, checked\n\n| Claim (report §5) | As reported | Like-for-like (my calculation from the raw files) |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Units up 22.4% on August\" | 7,488 → 9,162 units | September has 5 weekly buckets and August has 4. **Per week: 1,872 → 1,832, or −2.1%.** CRAFT demand fell slightly. |\n| \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units\" | NCR count ÷ units sold | The record count was flat (24 → 23), and the units dropped by one extra week. **Units affected rose 2,324 → 2,832 (+22%).** Units affected as a share of units sold stayed at **31.0% → 30.9%**. Quality did not improve. |\n| \"No other line improved\" | — | Also comes from the count-based rate. On units affected, CRAFT is flat like everyone else, and it is still roughly 3–6× the other lines (AURA 5.8%, PULSE 8.5%, NEST 10.5%). |\n\nOne more detail: the fifth September bucket is FY24-Q3-W01, which starts 2023-09-30. That means 6 of its 7 days are October. The report itself says in §6 that the +26% is mostly the extra week, but §5 quotes the +22.4% anyway.\n\n## Metric cards (short)\n\n**CRAFT defect rate, as reported**\n- Counts non-conformance records (NCRs), so number of records, per 1,000 units sold, September vs August.\n- Source: `ncr_records.csv` count plus `demand_actuals.csv`.\n- Limit: it counts records, not units, and the denominator has unequal periods. It drops whenever one more week of sales lands in the month.\n\n**CRAFT defect rate, by quantity**\n- Sum of `qty_affected` per unit sold, same months and sources.\n- Limit: production quantity divided by sales quantity, so it's an indicator, not a true yield. It's also production quality, not customer returns (report §6).\n\n**CRAFT demand growth**\n- Units sold per weekly bucket.\n- Limit: the September month total includes a Q3 week.\n\n**Trace check:** CRAFT September units affected = 2,832 = `ncr_records.csv`, `line=CRAFT`, `ncr_date` 2023-09-01 to 09-30, 23 rows, sum of `qty_affected`. That matches the report's §3 table. The 3.21 and 2.51 figures also recompute exactly (24/7,488 and 23/9,162), so the report's arithmetic is right. The definition is the problem.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n- **Decision:** Don't move Q3 marketing to CRAFT now. Fund the rework first.\n- **Why:** Measured by quantity, about **31% of units sold carry a quality hit**, in both months (2,324 and 2,832 units affected), and per-week demand is flat to slightly down (−2.1%). Pushing more demand would push more production through a process with the highest defect level of any line. September also had more scrap records than August (13 of 23 vs 11 of 24).\n- **Risk if you hold off:** CRAFT is €4.12M of list revenue, so a delay could leave premium demand on the table. That's limited by the fact that real margin is unknown, since there's no per-SKU cost (§6), and spend can't be attributed per line anyway (§4).\n- **Risk if you spend now:** More output on a line where about 3 in 10 units are flagged, plus scrap and rework cost on a €450 product, and possible damage to the premium brand.\n- **Counter-position:** On the report's definition (NCR count per 1,000 units), CRAFT \"improved\" by 22% (3.21 → 2.51). But that comes only from counting records instead of units and from the unequal month lengths.\n- **What would reverse this:** Units affected falls below about **15 per 100 CRAFT units sold** (half today's level) in two consecutive 4-week windows, **and** per-week CRAFT demand is at least flat over the same windows. I've stated this in units on purpose: a target based on NCR count could be met just by merging records, without anything changing on the line.\n\nThe 15-per-100 threshold is my suggestion, not a team standard. There was no team metric-card file to check it against, so you may want to set it with Quality.",
